---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const title = "Successful Transmission of 128-QAM SC-FDM — BONLAB";
const description = "BONLAB researchers successfully demonstrate 128-QAM SC-FDM transmission over optical wireless communications using real-time FPGA digital signal processing.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
<section class="detail-hero">

    <div class="container">

        <a href="news.html" class="back-link">
            ← Back to News
        </a>

        <span class="eyebrow">
            RESEARCH HIGHLIGHT · LAB UPDATE
        </span>

        <h1>
            Successful Transmission of 128-QAM SC-FDM over Optical Wireless Communications
        </h1>

        <p>
            Demonstrating high spectral efficiency and low peak-to-average power ratio through FPGA real-time DSP pipelines.
        </p>

    </div>

</section>


<section class="section">

    <div class="container article-layout">

        <article class="article-content">

            <img class="project-image" src="assets/research-128qam-scfdm.jpg" alt="128-QAM SC-FDM Constellation Display in BONLAB">

            <p class="article-lead">
                The Broadband Optical Network Laboratory (BON LAB) at RMUTI Khon Kaen Campus has achieved a key milestone in optical wireless communications (OWC), successfully demonstrating the transmission and real-time reception of <strong>128-QAM Single-Carrier Frequency Division Multiplexing (SC-FDM)</strong> signals.
            </p>

            <p>
                Under the leadership of <strong>Assoc. Prof. Dr. Kidsanapong Puntsri</strong>, the research team designed an end-to-end transceiver architecture combining DFT precoding, discrete constellation mapping, and carrier synchronization implemented on high-speed FPGA hardware boards.
            </p>

            <h3>Why 128-QAM SC-FDM Matters</h3>
            <p>
                In optical wireless communications, transmitter devices such as laser diodes (LDs) and LEDs exhibit nonlinear characteristics that severely degrade multicarrier signals with high Peak-to-Average Power Ratios (PAPR). SC-FDM effectively mitigates this issue while allowing high constellation densities (128-QAM), offering both energy efficiency and high throughput.
            </p>

            <h3>Laboratory Validation Setup</h3>
            <p>
                The validation setup at BONLAB utilizes arbitrary waveform generators (AWG), optical emitter modules, high-sensitivity photodetectors, and real-time digital oscilloscopes for EVM and BER verification.
            </p>

            <p>
                To explore further research and collaboration opportunities on optical communications and FPGA prototyping, visit our <a href="contact.html">Contact page</a> or our <a href="https://sites.google.com/site/adcsbonlab/home" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official Google Site</a>.
            </p>

        </article>


        <aside class="article-sidebar">

            <div class="sidebar-card">

                <h3>Article Details</h3>

                <div class="info-row">
                    <span>Category</span>
                    <strong>Research Milestone</strong>
                </div>

                <div class="info-row">
                    <span>Laboratory</span>
                    <strong>BON LAB (มทร.อีสาน)</strong>
                </div>

                <div class="info-row">
                    <span>Lead Investigator</span>
                    <strong>รศ.ดร.กฤษณะพงศ์ พันธ์ศรี</strong>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="sidebar-card">

                <h3>Follow BONLAB</h3>

                <p>
                    Stay up-to-date with our latest publications and activities:
                </p>

                <p><a class="text-link" href="https://web.facebook.com/profile.php?id=100076231050286" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Facebook Page →</a></p>
                <p><a class="text-link" href="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8698-5407" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ORCID Profile →</a></p>

            </div>

        </aside>

    </div>

</section>
</Layout>
